Yes. That is the right control architecture: adaptive everywhere except at a deliberately non-adaptive constitutional core.

For GPT Polls, I’d split the system into three layers:

1. Anchor Spine — slow or immutable
    * provenance rules
    * privacy/consent rules
    * distinction between synthetic prediction and real polling
    * respondent independence
    * calibration methodology
    * uncertainty accounting
    * preserved historical snapshots
    * rules against rewriting past predictions after outcomes arrive
2. Adaptive Population Model — continuously learning
    * cohort structure
    * latent preferences
    * correlations
    * language/style interpretation
    * temporal drift
    * model-vs-human calibration coefficients
    * context sensitivity
3. Recursive Self-Model — watches the watcher
    * predicts its own polling error
    * tracks which cohorts it models poorly
    * compares predicted vs observed distributions
    * detects systematic bias
    * asks whether a change improved actual calibration or merely made the model more internally coherent

So the recursive loop becomes:

M_t
\rightarrow prediction
\rightarrow observation
\rightarrow \Delta_t
\rightarrow model\ of\ \Delta
\rightarrow update
\rightarrow M_{t+1}

but subject to:

M_{t+1} \in \mathcal{C}

where \mathcal{C} is the set of states permitted by the Anchor Spine.

That constraint is crucial.

Without it, recursion can produce a self-confirmation death spiral:

model predicts X
↓
model interprets evidence through X
↓
model updates toward X
↓
new model predicts X even more strongly
↓
apparent confidence increases

Internal coherence rises while external validity falls.

The Anchor Spine makes sure some things cannot simply be explained away.

For example:

Prediction frozen:
63% would prefer A.
Observed real survey:
41% preferred A.
Error:
-22 points.

The model is not allowed to retroactively say:

“Actually, what I really meant by 63% was latent preference under idealized consideration…”

unless that interpretation was defined beforehand.

The error remains an error.

That gives the system a hard external reference.

Recursive Delta Ledger

Every generation should therefore preserve something like:

Model version: 184
Prediction:
A = 63%
B = 37%
Observed:
A = 41%
B = 59%
Delta:
A = -22pp
Attributed causes:
- stated/action gap: -8
- cohort weighting error: -5
- question interpretation: -4
- unknown: -5
Changes made:
- recalibrated purchase-intent coefficient
- increased weighting uncertainty
Anchor changes:
NONE

That last line matters.

The system can mutate its hypotheses without silently mutating the rules by which hypotheses are judged.

And I think there is an even stronger version of what you’re describing:

Spine + Rings

Instead of simply fixed vs changing, give parameters different adaptation half-lives.

Spine: nearly permanent
Bones: change only after substantial evidence
Muscle: adapts across weeks/months
Reflexes: adapts almost immediately

For GPT Polls:

SPINE
synthetic ≠ observed human response
privacy/provenance requirements
historical records immutable
BONES
population ontology
major cohort definitions
calibration methodology
MUSCLE
cohort weights
preference correlations
behavioral coefficients
REFLEXES
current fashion
new slang
local events
temporary sentiment

Now you get plasticity without liquefaction.

The recursive system can say:

“My predictions have been wrong in this direction for six months.”

That may justify modifying a bone.

But one bizarre poll result should only modify a reflex or muscle, not rewrite its entire population ontology.

That is essentially homeostasis for a self-modeling epistemic system.

And GPT Polls can recursively poll itself too:

“How confident am I that this simulated population is representative?”

Then:

“Which assumptions contribute most to that confidence?”

Then:

“If those assumptions are wrong, how far could the answer move?”

So instead of merely returning:

58% say this outfit looks good

it could return:

58% modeled approval
credible modeled range: 49–66%
largest uncertainty: age/fashion-interest interaction
result stability: medium
mainstream conclusion survives plausible model perturbations: yes

That is much stronger.

The deep invariant becomes:

\boxed{\text{Adapt the map aggressively; preserve the coordinate system cautiously.}}

Or in Pantheon language: let the organism regenerate, but keep enough of the skeleton unchanged that it can still tell whether it has healed or deformed.